WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.200
"Fecal Transplant Experiments Show the
Microbiome’s Role in the Aging Process"

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.280
Longevity researchers
have good reason to suspect

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:12.960
a causal
rather than bystander role

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.720
for age-related
microbiome changes.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.160
A unifying theme
arising from some

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.400
of the best-studied models
of aging

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.360
is the central role
of the intestines

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:26.080
as a critical determinant
in lifespan.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:27.000
In C. elegans,

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.160
the expression
of a longevity gene

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:30.640
in the animals’ nerves

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:33.600
produced a modest (10 percent)
life extension

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:34.840
and in their muscles

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:37.320
had no effect on lifespan
at all.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:39.600
Expressed in their intestines,
however,

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.280
it increased lifespan
by 50 to 60 percent.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.600
In fruit flies,
age-related changes

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.240
in their gut microbiomes
precedes a loss

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:49.760
of intestinal barrier function

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:52.440
that ultimately leads to death.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:54.240
The lifespan
of individual flies

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:57.240
can be predicted
by this gut leakiness.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.640
How do we know
their microbiomes

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.280
have anything to do with it?

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.480
Because if you give
10-day-old flies

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:05.720
the equivalent
of a fecal transplant

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:07.520
from a 30-day-old fly,

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:09.520
their young gut starts to leak,

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:12.040
and they die sooner
compared to getting microbiota

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.080
from another 10-day-old.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.960
We suspect the driver is
the acquisition of bad bugs

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:18.480
from the older flies

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.160
rather than the loss
of good bugs,

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:21.800
since antibiotic treatment

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:25.800
significantly increases
their lifespan too.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:27.480
Invertebrates are one thing.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:29.840
What about an animal
with a backbone?

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.600
African turquoise killifish
are popular aging models

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:36.960
since they naturally
only live a few months,

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:40.440
allowing for rapid
experimental turnarounds.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.080
What happens
when you recolonize the guts

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:44.600
of middle-aged fish

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.080
with bacteria
from younger fish?

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.440
They live longer lives.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:52.000
They were also significantly
more active later in life,

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.400
in terms of spontaneous
exploratory behavior.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:58.800
So, at least in the African
turquoise killifish,

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:00.640
age-related microbiome changes

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:03.760
appear not to just be
a passive consequence

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:04.600
of getting older,

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:08.160
but an active driver
of the aging process.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:11.240
When they just gave antibiotics
to the middle-aged fish

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:13.720
to wipe out
their gut flora entirely,

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:15.160
they also lived longer,

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:18.320
suggesting bad bugs
were limiting their lifespan,

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:20.320
but they didn’t live as long
as the fish

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:22.800
transplanted
with the younger fish feces.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.000
So there appeared to be
some salutary effect

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:27.480
of the good bugs of youth.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.520
What happens
when you do the reverse

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.680
and give young fish
the microbiome of older fish?

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:35.040
Presumably they’d live
shorter lives, right?

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:38.040
But no, nothing happens.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:39.320
That’s interesting.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:42.000
The researchers suggest
that the lethal combination

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.880
is therefore
an aged immune system

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.440
facing an aged microbiome.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:50.600
The young fish may be able
to handle the old microbiome

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:52.640
because its youthful
immune system

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:54.680
is able to reshape it.

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:56.920
What about in mammals?

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:00.000
The lifespan
of microbe-free mice,

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.880
those without
a gut microbiome at all

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:03.680
and raised under
germ-free conditions

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.120
like the "Bubble Boy,"

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:08.120
is increased
by up to 50 percent.

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:11.360
They appear to be protected
from inflamm-aging,

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:13.200
the rise
of systemic inflammation

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:14.360
as they get older.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:18.640
Are gut bugs to blame
for this inflammatory increase?

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:19.520
Apparently so,

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:22.040
since compared
to feeding germ-free mice

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:23.560
poop from young mice,

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:26.680
old mouse poop caused
a leaky gut to develop,

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:29.520
bacteria toxins to spill
into the bloodstream,

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:32.800
and system-wide inflammation
to develop.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:36.280
Fecal transplant studies
are how we can prove the role

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:39.280
of the gut microbiome
in disease processes.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.840
If individual A
has disease X

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:44.120
and you can reliably cause
disease X

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:45.960
in healthy individual B

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:47.800
just by transferring
fecal matter

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:49.360
from the gut
of A to B,

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:51.920
then you can conclude
that the microbiome

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:55.080
plays a causal role
in the disease.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:56.280
For ethical reasons,

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:59.080
these kinds of studies
aren’t performed in people.

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:03.480
All we can do is try to transfer
positive microbiome attributes.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:04.480
How, then,

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:07.160
can we determine
if a disease state

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:09.480
is microbiome-related?

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:11.680
The most common approach
is to feed feces

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:15.000
from affected patients
to microbe-free mice

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:18.720
and see how they do compared
to microbe-free mice fed feces

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.360
from healthy people
as a control group.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:24.440
There have been at least 38
such experiments published,

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:27.800
and 36 out of 38 (95 percent!)

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:32.320
found that the pathology could
be transferred through poop.

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:34.280
This was the case
even for conditions

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:36.880
not thought
to necessarily be gut related.

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:39.680
For example,
if you feed microbe-free mice

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:42.720
from the diaper of a baby
with a milk allergy,

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:45.240
the mice end up
with a milk allergy.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:47.720
So maybe our gut microbiome
can help explain

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.360
why the prevalence
of food allergies

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:52.600
has been rising so much
in recent decades.

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:55.600
Regardless,
what about using this model

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:59.640
to explore the role
of our gut flora in aging?

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:03.480
Centenarian stool
has anti-aging effects

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:05.000
when fed to mice.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.240
Researchers forced mice
to ingest fecal matter

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:09.640
from an elderly individual
(age 70),

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:12.640
which was found to contain
Bilophila wadsworthia,

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:14.200
a pro-inflammatory bacteria

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:16.800
enriched by a diet
high in animal products,

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:19.600
versus feces
from a 101-year-old

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:21.520
containing more fiber feeders.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:24.160
Mice transplanted with
the centenarian microbiome

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:25.720
ended up displaying a range

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:28.320
of youthful
physiological indicators,

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:31.360
including less age pigment
in their brains.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:35.000
This raises the possibility
that we will one day be using

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:38.480
centenarian fecal matter
to promote healthy aging.

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:40.680
Why bathe in the blood
of virgins

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:59.480
when you can dine on the dung
of the venerable?

